    "initialSymptom": {
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "the-clue-was-already-in-the-oil",
          "title": "The clue was already in the oil.",
          "description": "A deep rod-knock sound is not the same as normal diesel clatter. In this case, the owner also noticed metallic glitter in the oil � a serious warning sign for bearing-shell wear.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "image",
              "src": "/case-studies/assets/oil-glitter-macro.png",
              "alt": "Macro photograph of used engine oil showing copper and silver metallic glitter particles from BMW N57 crankshaft bearing failure",
              "width": "",
              "height": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Stage 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cold-start knock"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Deep rhythmic noise for around 10 seconds, then quieter once warm."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Stage 2"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Knock under load"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The sound became louder and more consistent during acceleration."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Stage 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Metal in oil"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Copper and silver glitter suggested bearing-shell material circulating through the oil."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If an N57 develops a deep bottom-end knock, do not keep driving it. Every journey can increase crankshaft journal scoring and reduce the chance of a repairable rebuild."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Failure timeline"
            }
          ],
          "links": [],
          "source": {
            "sectionId": "the-clue-was-already-in-the-oil",
            "order": 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "progression": {
      "items": [
        {
          "id": "5-000-miles-from-first-knock-to-recovery",
          "title": "5,000 miles from first knock to recovery.",
          "description": "The knock started around 128,000 miles and ended with a flashing engine management light at 133,000 miles. The car was recovered to a BMW specialist in Birmingham.",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "image",
              "src": "/case-studies/assets/timeline-bg-2.png",
              "alt": "BMW N57 failure timeline background showing 5,000 mile progression from first knock at 128,000 miles to catastrophic failure at 133,000 miles",
              "width": "",
              "height": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "What the owner was told"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "�Probably just the injectors settling.� The car still drove, so the warning was underestimated."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "What was actually happening"
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Bearing-shell wear was producing metal contamination and likely scoring the crankshaft journals."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "�The car still drove, but every journey sounded worse than the last.�"
            },
